article {


hgroup {
    h1 { AI 2026 }

    Where we are, and where we aren't
}
hr {}

section#intro {
It's October 2026. Or at least, it was when I began writing this. OpenAI has just released a[href=https://openai.com/index/sharing-ai-progress-in-mathematics/]{a massive github repo of .pdf and .lean files}.

There are more than 700 PDFs, and some portion of those results also come with Lean certificates.

This has led to quite a bit of discussion. Is it the end of human mathematics? Is it time to move to a "math 2.0"? Is AGI here? Or ASI? The eschaton?

With all the commentary flying around, much of it quite bombastic, I figured I should go ahead and make a record of where my head was at at this time. I don't intend this as a persuasive piece, more of a snapshot of my thoughts. I will not be proceeding from a set of plausible assumptions and attempting to establish all my takes as necessary consequences thereof, rather I will be circling around a number of interrelated themes and attempting to convey how things look from my perspective.

For those who follow me on bluesky and are pious enough to read all my posts every day, you will probably already have seen many of these takes, albeit with a little less polish.

While I was assembling these thoughts I ended up reading William Thurston's essay a[href=https://arxiv.org/abs/math/9404236]{On Proof and Progress in Mathematics}, and as it turns out much of what I've written is quite similar to what Thurston had already capably put to words before I was even born. While I hadn't read it myself before I wrote up the bulk of this, my thinking has doubtlessly been greatly influenced by it, albeit indirectly. If you only have time to read one essay today, I encourage you to go read that one.

I may continue to tack more takes onto this post but, to stay true to the title, I will stop doing so when 2027 rolls around.
}

