Is this AGI?

No. It's RL. There is much left to be desired when it comes to the em{generality} of today's models. Pretraining brings a certain em{initial} generality at least when it comes to the scope of the model's knowledge. RL environments, the predominant training methodology of this moment, have to be continually crafted to train models to do better on various benchmarks, and the models don't seem to be getting all that much better at things for which nobody has devised an RL environment yet. In a sense all the work being done to create these environments constitutes a new i{form} of manual encoding of knowledge; the encoding just does not consist of procedural instructions. The procedure is implicit in the environment and reward model.

I'll admit I don't really like the term $scare{AGI}, I think intelligence is a term that gestures at a whole array of phenomena, and its meaning is constantly under negotiation; much of what artificial intelligence companies do is take part in the societal negotiation of what intelligence should be taken to be. Insofar as the things we mean by $scare{intelligence} are real and important, I think RL has a lot of issues that keep it from capturing the gestalt in the way the term $scare{artificial general intelligence} would suggest. My sense at this time is that RL as an algorithm tends to lead to brittle policies, for one thing. For another, an RL agent will happily $scare{learn} to be stupid, if a[href=https://github.com/anthropics/political-neutrality-eval]{stupidity is what's being rewarded}. That is, you can reward any behavior you like and the RL process will optimize the agent toward maximizing that reward, and that extends to cases where you reward the model for promulgating outright falsehoods or, more subtly, lending excessive credence to views that are at odds with scientific consensus. $noted { RL will also tend to seek the route to reward that requires the least learning. $note In some cases this is called $scare{reward hacking} or $scare{specification gaming}. I dislike these terms, at once personifying and demonizing an optimization process in a way that doesn't lend any real understanding. They just seem loaded with ideological baggage, to me, and are akin to rolling a ball down a slope and accusing it of $scare{gravity hacking} if it ends up somewhere different from where one intended. } Actually learning something subtle and difficult is a last resort, to an RL model; they must be dragged kicking and screaming into the light. New RL environments are constantly being designed and redesigned to shape model behavior; but that design consists of choices, human judgement calls about em{what intelligence is} in any given environment. Which behavior is em{intelligent} and which is em{a hack} is a decision motivated by a particular human's understanding of what it means to be intelligent. Intelligence is not some number the labs are simply pushing ever upward. It is an idea that we as a culture continually litigate the nature of, and the training of these systems is not purely downstream of the outcome of that litigation: the choices made by $scare{artificial intelligence} labs about how to train their models are themselves em{part} of that litigation. Decisions made about what capabilities to prioritize when making a product they declare to be em{intelligence} constitute assertions about what it means to be intelligent, regardless of whether the people making those decisions are conscious of that aspect of what they're doing, and regardless of what pragmatic concerns are involved in the decision-making process.

Some accuse those who deny that this is AGI of $scare{moving the goalposts}; but I have been consistent in my time on this Earth in my insistence that there is much more to a mind than optimizing an objective function, chasing a reward function defined by another, or to put it bluntly i{making number go up}.

One litmus test for a widely-used technology being dubbed $scare{AGI} should be: is its integration into society making that society more generally intelligent? Are the people that use AI the most behaving in a more $scare{generally intelligent} way? A world where anyone can have a machine print out a Lean proof they don't understand to answer a question they don't understand, while we still wage wars and burn fossil fuels, while men who assert patriarchy to be the will of the divine seize control of political institutions, where discredited racist pseudoscience makes a comeback among those that style themselves $scare{rationalists}, where ICE rounds people up to put in camps and we send bombs halfway around the world to be dropped on schools and hospitals. All this shit isn't $scare{orthogonal} to intelligence: Misogyny is stupid. Fascism is stupid. Racism is fucking stupid. This moment in history is so goddamn fucking stupid, and sadly it doesn't seem like AI is helping correct for that to any significant degree. This is an age of rampant epistemic vice, and some of those who revel most wildly in such vice are enthusiastic adopters of AI. It is making them more effective at rapidly accomplishing tasks in certain domains, but it is not markedly improving their general ability to understand the world they live in, take established science seriously, place political currents in continuity with their histories, think at a broader scale than personal greed, escape perverse incentives, &c &c. The models aren't being trained to help people do any of that, because that's not what the labs understand to be part of intelligence. The major US labs are taken with the ideology that all of that amounts to em{values}, entirely unrelated to intelligence. But their devotion to neutrality goes a[href=https://github.com/anthropics/political-neutrality-eval/blob/main/eval_set.csv]{far beyond up-in-the-air matters of ethical philosophy, and extends to training their models not to take sides on matters of settled science when doing so would upset the political right}.


tools should help us be better thinkers, and certainly shouldn't make us *feel* like better thinkers while failing to do so

$footnotes { $scare{Is this AGI?} Footnotes }

---





---

A mathematician is uniquely qualified to say why anyone would give a shit about any of these results




