quote from sanderson on tao's blog:
"One clear shortcoming of a motivated explanation is that its validity is not binary the way a proof’s is. This is a big reason proof is so useful a way to measure progress: You can clearly define what does and does not have a proof yet. There will never be Lean for motivated explanations."

this is not a shortcoming of motivated explanation. this is a shortcoming in the ideological expectation that everything should be evaluable by "objective" binary judgements






from thurston's proof & progress essay:
"Basic concepts used every day within one subfield are
often foreign to another subfield. Mathematicians give up on trying to understand
the basic concepts even from neighboring subfields, unless they were clued in as
graduate students."
this is something i think AI can help quite a bit to address!









the oai proofs are basically empirical data for ppl to pick apart & theorize about if they are interested in doing so; math, in its pluralistic nature, need not change anything about itself to encompass such pursuits

arguably such pursuits belong more to computer science than to mathematics, but i'm of the opinion that compsci is a subfield of math anyway



maybe cut or reduce this bit, it is slightly breaking the vow

I've made a vow never to make a long-form post about vibecoding, but this post is about AI more broadly so I feel it's fine to say a few words on it: How much benefit is there, really, in getting swept up in feelings of grandiose potential all while you have to keep paying 200 dollars a month to companies run by reactionary billionaires in order to feel able to continue work on your software projects? As far as I can see there hasn't been any corresponding explosion in the quantity of good software out there. It often seems as if those experiencing the feelings of power and potential forgotten that people could already do impressive things with code em{before} AI hit the scene. Software engineers have always been quick to become die-hard evangelists for new tools and much of the talk about LLMs has the same character as can be seen in other SWE fads of the past and present.

On the other hand you have the $scare{AI is never good for anything} types. I think that's also a ridiculous position. There are plenty of things AI is useful for. Even the free tier models are useful.

A lot of people tie their identity up in either their embrace or rejection of AI, and I think that's a tendency one ought to make a conscious effort to overcome in either case.











Formalization isn't em{what this process of proof $scare{really is,}} it's em{an attempt at precisely specifying it}. Formal systems have properties that make them extremely useful in mathematics, especially when it comes to communicating rigorous proofs; formal systems are themselves a subject of mathematical inquiry, and learning about them is vital to a mathematical education. However they are not the foundation of all mathematical practice. They are em{especially} unsuited to form an epistemological foundation, for reasons both philosophical and technical.


Now my point isn't $scare{you really should believe that the square root of two is irrational and really shouldn't believe the OAI N-S proof}, rather it's to gesture at there being a rich world of epistemic and phenomenological diversity among the things we call $scare{proofs}.






you can encode group theoretic axioms in zfc, that doesn't mean that group theorists are all "using zfc", any more than it means they're all using any other foundation that could encode their work.








If we had models being trained basically exactly the same way as OpenAI's but the opt-in $scare{train on my shit} checkbox was off by default and users could have a greater regulatory assurance it was even being respected, and this training was being done by an institution controlled by the public, accessible as a public service, it'd actually be uncomplicatedly cool as hell. Everything everyone is working on instantly absorbed into a common pool of knowledge accessible to all, as fast as they can develop their ideas. That's utopian! What's not utopian is it being beholden to Sam Altman and shareholders, a private company inclined to try to compete with its users and claim credit for what is effectively a collective achievement. It's dystopian that access to such a system is gated behind a pricey subscription model or even pricier business-to-business bullshit, it being endlessly marketed as the soon-to-be replacement for all intellectual labor, or perhaps em{all labor}, and as having transcended through scale alone its nature as an RL-tuned LLM, as having undergone the mysterious phenomenon of $scare{emergence} and become something ineffably em{more} (but no, we're not going to make that rigorous) -- is just tedious!


